A||||21||fieroluke||New instrument faceplates||||3||
Z||000000||fieroluke||06-21-2001||05:18 AM||||Hi again,<P>I was surfing the web and found that homemade speedo faceplates are really popular with the Volkswagen crowd. They seem to print on photographic paper (thick, like plastic) with an inkjet, and from the pictures, it looks good. We do have a color laser here at work, so I may try that one too. They recommend adding the orange backlight by glueing orange foil behind the paper. I was thinking of airbrushing the paper from behind with orange paint. My only concern is how well the inkjet paint will stand up to fading. I'll try printing the b/w gauges with my b/w laser as well, and compare the results. And if all else fails, I can ask my dad to print it on his offset printing machine. But that's a last resort...<P>Anyway, does anyone have any helpful hints, or has anyone tried something similar? Any comments would be welcome before I get started, to keep me from trying stuff that has been tried before.<P>I don't feel like spending $100 for a set of gauge faceplates if I can print them myself for $1. Besides, I want some features nobody else offers...<P>Things I'd like to do:<P>- recalibrate my speedo to read km/h (I know, "get a Canadian speedo", but they're hard to find for a GT)<BR>- raise the max. from 200km/h to 240km/h (~140mph) (c'mon: 200mph/330km/h looks STUPID)<BR>- Make coolant temp read Celsius while keeping the E and F symbols for the fuel gauge<BR>- Make a backlit tach with the oil-pressure style gauge in tach reading MPG (fuel prices in Germany are exceeding $3.80 a gallon :-( , so some kind of fuel economy indicator is desparately needed)<P>I'll keep you posted of my progress...<P>Best regards,<P>-Oliver<P>------------------<BR>Visit my website: <A HREF="http://www.fieros.de" TARGET=_blank>www.fieros.de</A>  for lots of technical and other Fiero related information<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fieros.de/jpg/mycars.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by fieroluke (edited 06-21-2001).]||||reg||3
Z||000001||Slammed Fiero||06-21-2001||10:21 AM|||| <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Mvc-065f_1.jpg"> <P>These are mine (canadian) I added 1000rpm to the Tach and the lotus V* crest. If you use the search function AUS fiero pretty much outlined and posted everything you need to make a set of backlite units. Mine use 3M Durel 3 Electroluminescient sheet (fancy name for indiglow) instead of backlighting.<P><BR>I have all the faces on CD if you want a copy I can set up a site for you to Download them off of.<P>Hope this helps<P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1607505&a=12259132&p=48230089&Sequence=0&res=high">      <BR>Jonathan McCreery<BR>86 GT 4spd<BR>Koni's , Coilovers, Big Bars , 17" Trmotorsports Typhoons , 11.25" vented discs, Wilwood 4 Piston Calipers , Pirelli P Zero's, Short Shift, No cat , Cold air induction , Geni Stainless tips, White face gauges , Momo shift , momo pedals. &lt;b&gt; New White Face Gauges , 75Hp Shot of NOS&lt;/b&gt;Autocrossed , Tracked , Dragged? (soon) abused.||||reg||1
Z||000002||fieroluke||06-21-2001||12:09 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Slammed Fiero:<BR><B>  <BR>These are mine (canadian) I added 1000rpm to the Tach and the lotus V* crest. If you use the search function AUS fiero pretty much outlined and posted everything you need to make a set of backlite units. Mine use 3M Durel 3 Electroluminescient sheet (fancy name for indiglow) instead of backlighting.<P><BR>I have all the faces on CD if you want a copy I can set up a site for you to Download them off of.<P>Hope this helps<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Yes, I'd really appreciate that!<BR>...but I'm too stupid to find the post you're referring to. Was that a post by AUSfiero? I found his posts, but none applied to making gauge faceplates, as far as I can see...<P>Thanks,<P>-Oliver<P><BR>------------------<BR>Visit my website: <A HREF="http://www.fieros.de" TARGET=_blank>www.fieros.de</A>  for lots of technical and other Fiero related information<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fieros.de/jpg/mycars.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by fieroluke (edited 06-21-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||FieroMaster88||06-21-2001||12:30 PM||||So you used that 3M stuff for your gauges????  How did that work out?  I was lookin at using that but was not sure how it worked.  <P>------------------<BR>        <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/FieroMaster88.gif">        <BR>James Essar<BR>AOL IM Name: FieroMaster88<BR>88 Coupe 30,000 miles  <IMG SRC="http://cwm.ragesofsanity.com/s/net4/smiling2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||rogergarrison||06-21-2001||12:34 PM||||I dont think 200 mph looks stupid i have one and an 8000 rpm tach. A 160 speedo wont work cause mine goes way over that, my redline is 7300. and thats only because of valve float.<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1234884&a=9116379&p=34556781"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||3fifty||06-21-2001||01:37 PM||||How about the faces for us poor 85mph speedo<BR>folks?<P>3fifty one day !!!!!||||reg||5
Z||000006||87GTZ34||06-21-2001||02:45 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>...but I'm too stupid to find the post you're referring to. Was that a post by AUSfiero? I found his posts, but none applied to making gauge faceplates, as far as I can see...<BR><HR></BLOCKQUOTE> <P>Here's the link. Quite informative - thanks again Aus<BR> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/007977.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/007977.html</A> <P>||||reg||1
Z||000007||FieroBUZZ||06-21-2001||02:46 PM||||Oliver: Here is the thread you need.  AusFiero has kindly done the work for us. <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/007977.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/007977.html</A> <P>Aus:  Many thanks for these, they're on the things to do list for sure.  We KPH guys don't get a lot of freebies.<P>Gary||||reg||1
Z||000008||GT-maniac||06-21-2001||02:47 PM||||<P><p>[This message has been edited by GT-maniac (edited 06-21-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||fieroluke||06-21-2001||04:12 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by rogergarrison:<BR><B>I dont think 200 mph looks stupid i have one and an 8000 rpm tach. A 160 speedo wont work cause mine goes way over that, my redline is 7300. and thats only because of valve float.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Hi Roger,<P>you have a KitCar, that's different. In a Ferrari (or lookalike) people believe that car can do 200mph. But in a Fiero, oh please. I have the 200mph speedo in one of my Fieros, and even with "no speed limit" on the German highways, I hardly use half of the scale (usually 100-110mph, not more). If you have a different engine and can do more, I can understand it, but still, unless you race the car, where would you have use for that?<P>Didn't mean to offend anyone...<P>-Oliver||||reg||1
Z||000010||rogergarrison||06-21-2001||10:08 PM||||no offense taken Oliver. Its just the fact of 'knowing you can' I have occasionally wound it up on open straight freeway when theres no traffic. I have radar detector, CB radio, and police scanner to listen for the aircraft patrols.<P>I think if you use an inkjet photo cartridge and photopaper, it should last quite a while. It really doesnt get all the direct sunlight on it.<p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 06-21-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||fieroluke||06-22-2001||08:32 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by rogergarrison:<BR><B>no offense taken Oliver. Its just the fact of 'knowing you can' I have occasionally wound it up on open straight freeway when theres no traffic. I have radar detector, CB radio, and police scanner to listen for the aircraft patrols.<P>I think if you use an inkjet photo cartridge and photopaper, it should last quite a while. It really doesnt get all the direct sunlight on it.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Fortunately they don't have airborne speed controls over here yet, but our problem is rather that even if you're 1-2 mph faster than the limit, they fine you. Just $20 or so, but there's nothing you can do about it. I mean, fighting it is more expensive than just paying, so it is a way for the local government to make lots of money with little effort. I don't mind speed limits, and I don't mind paying if I speeded, but they don't enforce it for safety's sake or they'd be checking in front of schools instead of busy streets. *sigh*<P>Regarding the gauges, yes, I also think the ink should hold up well. My Dad has a printing machine, but he hasn't used it in years, so he'd need to get the chemicals to make offset plates again. But that would probably be a durable alternative. A whole set of gauges should fit on a single sheet of paper (or rather plastic, or whatever one should use), so once that's set up, making more shouldn't be overly expensive.<P>I'll check back with the forum if we've done tests, and maybe I'll be able to find some more interested people...<P>Best regards,<P>-Oliver<P>------------------<BR>Visit my website: <A HREF="http://www.fieros.de" TARGET=_blank>www.fieros.de</A>  for lots of technical and other Fiero related information<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fieros.de/jpg/mycars.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000012||Fleabitdog||06-22-2001||12:29 PM||||Fieromaster88<BR>  Did you forget about me?||||reg||1
Z||000013||FieroJET||06-22-2001||11:59 PM||||I am very interested in the gauges.  I have a Kodak Thermal Dye XLS 8600 printer.  If there would be a way to do this on transparency sheets or thin plastic I would let the faces go at what it costs me to print em'.  Would it be possible to do the face on clear plastic and piant the back white? Just a thought.||||reg||3
Z||000014||DandRauto||06-25-2001||01:03 PM||||I found the refered to site for the faces from AUSFIERO, but how do you print them.  I did a print of this frame and the gauges are about twice the size.  Also the aux gauges are cutoff on the right side even when printed in landscape.  Again how do you print them and get the right size?||||reg||1
Z||000015||Tweek||06-25-2001||01:21 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by DandRauto:<BR><B>I found the refered to site for the faces from AUSFIERO, but how do you print them.  I did a print of this frame and the gauges are about twice the size.  Also the aux gauges are cutoff on the right side even when printed in landscape.  Again how do you print them and get the right size?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I had that problem myself, besides all the other editing I did, I just measured how big they should be, and resized the images... sorry I cant remember how big off hand, but the is what the image looked like when I was done with it.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.oswg.net/Ted/Speedo4.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000016||DandRauto||06-25-2001||09:39 PM||||Ok , You did a nice job with the emblem.  So if I copy the image from these pages, I can place them in another program and then resize them.  Is there a preferred program to use and can the pieces be broken apart for editing.  I pasted it into word and was able to resize but not edit the pieces.||||reg||1
Z||000017||3fifty||06-26-2001||12:55 AM||||I printed and installed Tweeks gauges today.<BR>They were perfect in size. I need the aux set though. Aus's aux gauge printed way to big. I used Avery single page labels. If you<BR>cut the center hole just like the template, you can slide it over the needles. Tip of the day!!!!!!! Put a litthe dish washing liquid ect. on the gauge before you trey to slide the sticker on. I touched the speedo sticker by accident, and it was there to stay. The Avery stuff is so good it started to rip when I tried to pull it back up.||||reg||10
Z||000018||fieroluke||06-26-2001||05:12 AM||||I printed the images, and they were way too small, and blowing them up makes the images look coarse.<BR>So I scanned the 120mph speedo at 600dpi, did a little editing and printed it again on my Laserjet. Not bad! Instand 240 km/h speedo, and looks neat too!<BR>This is just paper, mind you, just to see how it looks. The toner looks a little rough though (I went with the original style, and large areas are not always 100% covered), but when the speedo is installed, you don't notice this. Next I'll try the same thing with color, and photo paper. I'll keep you posted.<P>Best regards,<P>-Oliver||||reg||14
Z||000019||Tweek||06-26-2001||10:21 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 3fifty:<BR><B>I printed and installed Tweeks gauges today.<BR>They were perfect in size. I need the aux set though. Aus's aux gauge printed way to big. I used Avery single page labels. If you<BR>cut the center hole just like the template, you can slide it over the needles. Tip of the day!!!!!!! Put a litthe dish washing liquid ect. on the gauge before you trey to slide the sticker on. I touched the speedo sticker by accident, and it was there to stay. The Avery stuff is so good it started to rip when I tried to pull it back up.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Uh oh...   lol Im redoing mine again (I didnt like the quality of some of em, esp. the tach.) I hope I can get the stuff back Up. I used Avery as well...  Oh well, I'll post what they look like when Im done, should be slightly better.<P>And Yep, I did notice you can slide it over, even the middle ones if you do it right. I'll see if I can ajust the aux ones too..||||reg||1
Z||000020||FieroJET||06-28-2001||01:10 AM||||I took and tried to make the prints (AUS's) on my thermal dye printer.  They look great just too big.  So I pulled my clusters out ripped the faces off and scanned them in.  Got the right size and everything but when I had them printed and installed them somethings not right.  The backlighting does not show through in some areas and is too bright in others.  Are you all making overlays or are you replacing the whole face? If interested I can send the layouts I have.  Full 8 x 9 sheet MPH, E,F,(fule),degrees F,PSI faces w/pontiac racing emblems (300 DPI).||||reg||7
Z||000021||Tweek||06-28-2001||05:17 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by FieroJET:<BR><B>I took and tried to make the prints (AUS's) on my thermal dye printer.  They look great just too big.  So I pulled my clusters out ripped the faces off and scanned them in.  Got the right size and everything but when I had them printed and installed them somethings not right.  The backlighting does not show through in some areas and is too bright in others.  Are you all making overlays or are you replacing the whole face? If interested I can send the layouts I have.  Full 8 x 9 sheet MPH, E,F,(fule),degrees F,PSI faces w/pontiac racing emblems (300 DPI).</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Thanks for the offer, but I think Im all set. I was just making overlays, plus mine are lit by the reflectorized stuff. Kinda strange, sometimes I have problems with printing sizes, if I open the image in IE or MSPaint, it prints perfect, but If I open it in photoshop or paint shop then its too large. Oh well. this is what I finished up with, and I installed them decently too. The pic here are half sized jpegs, links to full size bmp are below.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.oswg.net/Ted/gauges/speedosmall.jpg"> <P> <IMG SRC="http://www.oswg.net/Ted/gauges/tempandgassmall.jpg"> <P> <IMG SRC="http://www.oswg.net/Ted/gauges/tachsmall.jpg"> <P><BR>full size pics:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.oswg.net/Ted/gauges/speedo.bmp" TARGET=_blank>http://www.oswg.net/Ted/gauges/speedo.bmp</A>  <A HREF="http://www.oswg.net/Ted/gauges/tempandgas.bmp" TARGET=_blank>http://www.oswg.net/Ted/gauges/tempandgas.bmp</A>  <A HREF="http://www.oswg.net/Ted/gauges/tach.bmp" TARGET=_blank>http://www.oswg.net/Ted/gauges/tach.bmp</A> ||||reg||1
